# Tutorial 25 — JES Entities, Input and Widgets
# Reference: docs/guides/jes-by-example.md
# Read scripts/tutorial/README.md for the complete course and linked references.
@scenario {{SCENARIO_PREFIX}}_tutorial_25
@include {{CHARACTERS_INCLUDE}}
{{BG_DECL}}@label start
[mode dialogue standard]
[set ui.dialogueUi project]
[set ui.textBoxAsset none]
[set ui.continueIndicator spinning_arrow]
[if tutorial_still]
[set ui.reduceMotion true]
[else]
[set ui.reduceMotion false]
[endif]
[if !tutorial_initialized]
[set tutorial_count 0]
[flag tutorial_initialized]
[endif]
[set ui.characterHeightFactor 1.5]
[set ui.characterBaselineY 1.5]
[set character.lavender.imageEffect none]
{{BG_START}}
[textspeed 28]
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: JES is the scene language for entities, components, input bindings, camera work, and timelines. Java handlers supply behavior that needs game logic.
@label lesson_menu
> Visit the runnable JES studio -> scene
> Understand scene composition -> components
> Add controls and behaviors -> practice
> Checkpoint -> checkpoint
> Finish and return to the hub -> return_hub
@label scene
Lavender: The studio uses Panel2D and Label2D, a parallel timeline, and keyboard return handlers. Press Enter or Escape to come back.
[jes push {{JES_SCENE_PATH}} label scene_return with source=workshop]
@label scene_return
Lavender: A pushed scene returned to this label. The VNS conversation is still here.
[jump lesson_menu]
@label components
Lavender: An entity has a name and components. Panel2D draws a colored region; Label2D draws text; Sprite2D displays an image. Transform and hierarchy determine where they appear.
Lavender: Sprite animation, particles, camera motion, and parallel timeline tracks can combine into a scene. Keep names stable because scripts and handlers refer to them.
Lavender: In {b}scripts/scenes/studio_tour.jes{/b}, change the title, its coordinates, and the motion duration. Run it again and compare the result.
[jump lesson_menu]
@label practice
Lavender: Button2D, Slider2D, and other UI components expose callback names. Register the matching Java call handlers before running a scene that depends on them.
Lavender: Exercise: add a Button2D using the existing {b}return{/b} handler, with readable normal, hover, and pressed colors. Make keyboard return work too.
Lavender: The interactive-UI guide then shows a settings panel with live values and callbacks. Follow that example when you need actual mutable game logic.
Lavender: Screen coordinates, camera coordinates, and parent-relative transforms are different spaces. Debug a misplaced control by checking which space its component uses.
[jump lesson_menu]
@label checkpoint
Lavender: A button names a custom callback. What else must exist?
> A registered handler for that callback -> correct
> Nothing; its label becomes executable logic -> retry
@label retry
Lavender: A label is text. The named handler provides the behavior.
[jump checkpoint]
@label correct
Lavender: Right. Keep visual composition and executable behavior connected by explicit names.
[jump return_hub]

@label return_hub
[if !tutorial_done_25]
[flag tutorial_done_25]
[inc tutorial_count]
[endif]
[goto {{HUB_TARGET}}:topics_page_5]
